{
  "scenario_id": "sc-409000",
  "overall_assessment": "anomalies",
  "findings": [
    {
      "benefit": "IP",
      "years": null,
      "pattern": "recovery",
      "direction": "increase",
      "magnitude": "Mental Health termination A/E ~1.99 (terminations ~2x the assumed recovery rate; z ~ +50 on ~2,544 expected recoveries), lifting overall IP termination A/E to 1.237 while IP claim A/E stays 1.002",
      "confidence": 0.99,
      "evidence": [
        "artifacts/ae_ip_termination.csv: Mental Health pooled A/E 1.99 vs every other diagnosis 0.974-1.017 (z <= 1.0); effect is diagnosis-specific, not book-wide",
        "summary.json: overall_termination_ae 1.2371 with overall IP claim A/E 1.0023 and IP by-year A/E flat at 0.978-1.029 (no drift, overdispersion x0.68 vs Poisson)",
        "Death, CI and TPD by-year A/E all within ~5% of 1.0 with flat trends (max |z| 2.8 in 2016 Death, reverts next year; overdispersion <= x2.3; no cross-line coordinated swings) - the rest of the book is consistent with noise; the only material anomaly is the Mental Health recovery rate"
      ],
      "recommended_action": "Confirm persistence before changing the assumption: split the Mental Health termination A/E by claim vintage/month and by duration month (ae_ip_termination_by_month.csv) to check it is not a single vintage or a duration-cell artefact; investigate administrative or clinical causes (changes to recovery definitions, case management, treatment improvements, or reclassification of MH claims). If the ~2x recovery rate is sustained, reset the Mental Health recovery-rate assumption (shorter average claim duration) and reflect it in reserving and the pricing basis. Variance is favourable, so verify it is genuine experience rather than a one-off data/admin effect before booking it."
    }
  ]
}

